IA-64 EFI port
==============

Build with an IA-64 ELF GNU toolchain that still supports Itanium (for
example kernel.org's GCC 12.2 nolibc cross compiler and binutils 2.39):

  make CROSS=/path/to/ia64-linux- bin-ia64-efi/snp.efi
  make CROSS=/path/to/ia64-linux- bin-ia64-efi/intel.efi
  make CROSS=/path/to/ia64-linux- bin-ia64-efi/tests.efi

The standard deployment is SNP: iPXE uses EFI firmware's network driver.
intel.efi is a native-driver alternative and an emulator bootstrap fixture.
This target advertises DHCP architecture 2 (EFI Itanium), not x86-64.

EFI PE32+ entry is a two-word descriptor (code address, GP). The GNU-EFI
startup and relocator were imported from ncroxon/gnu-efi revision
87162316d1d04a12d5cc54f77bb7df117b73b146. Their original Hewlett-Packard
BSD notices are retained. The linker layout is adapted from the same
project's IA-64 EFI linker script. setjmp.S is adapted from GNU-EFI's
Intel BSD implementation: the return branch is saved explicitly and a
zero longjmp value is converted to one. GNU assembler inserts instruction
group stops in automatic dependency mode.

Local changes canonicalize relocated function descriptors by address.
This preserves C function-pointer equality, including aliases; iPXE uses
function pointers as interface operation IDs. A dedicated self-test also
checks indirect calls and globals through both descriptors.

This is an unsigned experimental port. EFI application chaining is
verified; no OS-specific IA-64 ELF loader or complete installation is
claimed. There is no physical Itanium hardware qualification yet.
